"""Human-authored complete-field observations, frozen before boundary serialization."""
NOTES = {
('abudawud:2385','hi'): "Jabir reports Umar's own fasting/kiss incident and his question to the Messenger. The rinse analogy, Umar's no-harm reply and final no-problem answer are all supplied. The parenthetical 'then what happened' is the finite rhetorical consequence of the question, not a word definition, and belongs to the dialogue. Retain the actual mixed interlocutors within one reported incident.",
('abudawud:2385','ur'): "The same own Urdu field explicitly has Umar kissing while fasting, asking the Messenger, the mouth-rinse hypothetical, Umar's response and the final answer. '( تو کیا ہوا )' completes the actual rhetorical question and is retained. Do not flatten Umar's quoted confession into the Messenger's words.",
('abudawud:2403','hi'): "Hamzah describes his travel/rental animals, youth, Ramadan and preference to fast without leaving a debt, then asks which choice is more rewarded. The Messenger permits his choice. Read the entire supplied question and its practical circumstances, including the printed Ramadan identification; no external journey details are supplied.",
('abudawud:2403','ur'): "Hamzah's full Urdu question includes transporting/renting mounts, encountering Ramadan, physical ability, deferred fasting as a debt, and the reward question. The response names Hamzah and leaves the choice to him. This is a complete reported dialogue without compiler tail.",
('abudawud:2405','hi'): "Anas reports travelling with the Messenger in Ramadan, some fasting and some not, and mutual absence of reproach. It is a narrated practice/observation, not a direct Prophetic quotation. All wording is present and retained without requiring a speech marker.",
('abudawud:2405','ur'): "The Urdu source supplies Anas's collective journey observation and both directions of non-reproach between fasters and nonfasters. Preserve its Companion narrative attribution and the explicit Ramadan/travel domain.",
('abudawud:2409','hi'): "Abu Darda describes a campaign, severe heat, hands/palms over heads, and only the Messenger and Abdullah ibn Rawahah fasting. Preserve the alternative hand/palm wording and the two named exceptions, without inferring the campaign's identity.",
('abudawud:2409','ur'): "This is Abu al-Darda's heat/campaign observation with the same printed two fasting exceptions. Every clause is narrative. The campaign is unnamed and remains so; the actual source names are preserved.",
('abudawud:2417','hi'): "Abu Sa'id reports three paired prohibitions: two feast-day fasts, two wrapping/seating dress forms, and prayer after dawn/afternoon. The parenthesis explaining the risk of exposing the private part is explanatory apparatus; retain it as context while keeping the two garments and prayer times in the report.",
('abudawud:2417','ur'): "The Urdu field supplies all six prohibition objects. The two numbered footnote glyphs attached to the garment words and the exposure-risk parenthesis are source apparatus/context, not additional quoted instructions. Preserve the source garment terms and every stated time/day.",
('abudawud:2423','hi'): "Complete field read: the sole content is Ibn Shihab's reaction to mention of the Saturday-fast prohibition, calling it a Hims people's hadith. This is criticism/provenance commentary about an absent report, rather than its body. Initial disposition hypothesis is source-only under the exclusion of explanatory/transmission apparatus, despite the numbered occurrence and named speaker. Read all other languages before committing that disposition.",
('abudawud:2423','ur'): "Complete Urdu field read: it reports Ibn Shihab calling the referenced fasting tradition Himsi, with a Saturday synonym and footnote marker. No actual independent fasting report body or attributed prohibition speaker is supplied. Preserve the whole field as provenance-commentary source context; the name/number alone is not admission evidence.",
('abudawud:2423','ar'): "Read the complete chain Abd al-Malik/Shuayb, Ibn Wahb, al-Layth to Ibn Shihab and the repeated response 'this is a Himsi hadith' when the Saturday prohibition was mentioned. The supplied statement is source criticism/provenance, not a quotation of that underlying report. No omitted prohibition body is reconstructed.",
('abudawud:2423','en'): "Read the entire English field: Al-Laith narrates al-Zuhri's response to mention of prohibited Saturday fasting, 'This is a Himsi tradition.' The content concerns the tradition's provenance; no Prophetic speaker or underlying report text is provided. The initial source-context decision applies to this full field too.",
('abudawud:2423','ur-Latn'): "Read all Roman Urdu: named Ibn Shihab's reaction, Saturday synonym, and Hims provenance assertion only. It gives no additional report body. All five own fields were examined before the initial source-only classification; this is not an unread-sibling exclusion.",
('abudawud:2426','hi'): "The prefix is a same-report/chain pointer through Abu Qatadah, but the field then prints a finite question about Monday and Thursday and a first-person birth/revelation clause. The Hindi omits the intervening 'he replied' and explicit respondent link found in other languages; preserve that absence. Keep the actual question and affirmative clause as separately framed abbreviated units in one transmitted variant, with the latter's ultimate speaker unresolved from this field. The Monday synonym is lexical context.",
('abudawud:2426','ur'): "After the same-chain pointer, Urdu gives the question about Monday/Thursday and explicitly says the Messenger replied that he was born and received Quran on that day. Retain those finite words with the source's attribution, without identifying an unstated referent day by borrowing another occurrence. The omitted original report is not filled.",
('abudawud:2456','hi'): "Umm Hani narrates conquest-day seating, Fatimah's left side and her right side, a drink passed from the Messenger to her, and her question after breaking a fast. The repayment question and voluntary-fast condition are complete. Retain the whole incident and its exact side/fasting distinctions.",
('abudawud:2456','ur'): "Urdu independently provides the same seating/drink/fast dialogue and explicit if-voluntary no-harm condition. Retain all narrative words; the final numbered footnote marker alone is context. No identification of the drink or servant is added.",
('tirmidhi:2382','hi'): "Read the entire shortened Hindi account: Shufayya meets Abu Hurayrah, requests a directly heard report, observes his distress, and receives the concise judgment-of-reciter/martyr/wealthy account and knee gesture. This language omits the long Urdu dialogues and Muawiyah reception; do not import them. The knee synonym is a lexical gloss outside selected narration.",
('tirmidhi:2382','ur'): "Read all 4,533 characters. Preserve the full Shufayya/Abu Hurayrah setting, repeated fainting, three judgment dialogues and final knee gesture in its own report family. After the compiler introduction, Walid/Abu Uthman/Ukbah affirmatively report Shufayya notifying Muawiyah; Alaa separately describes Muawiyah's response, crying, recovery and Quran recitation. Those finite historical reports must be retained under their actual transmitters/Muawiyah, with a separate reception family and distinct ordered components. Keep actual Arabic recitation and its Urdu rendering; remove only the Quran-study explanatory parenthesis, explicit Hud locator and final grade. No Muawiyah speech becomes Prophetic speech.",
('tirmidhi:2392','hi'): "Miqdam's attributed instruction says to inform a Muslim brother of one's love. Retain that instruction and its source framing; the following Tirmidhi grading and references to Abu Dharr/Anas parallels are compiler apparatus, not additional report bodies.",
('tirmidhi:2392','ur'): "Read the entire Urdu instruction and compiler tail. The finite instruction is supported through Miqdam; the numbered footnote and two numbered compiler notes remain context. Parallel names do not supply additional hadith text or establish canonical identity.",
('tirmidhi:2397','hi'): "Abu Wa'il/Shqiq transmits Aishah's observation that she saw no one's pain exceed the Messenger's. Retain Aishah's comparative observation under her actual speaker, not as words spoken by the Messenger. The final grade is context.",
('tirmidhi:2397','ur'): "The Urdu field gives the same Aishah observation through Abu Wa'il with a final footnote marker and Tirmidhi grade. The report body is Aishah's description; preserve her attribution and keep the marker/grade outside it.",
('tirmidhi:2406','hi'): "Ukbah ibn Amir asks about salvation and receives three instructions: control the tongue, stay within the house's capacity, and weep over faults. The actual question and all three instructions are retained. The final Tirmidhi grade is source context.",
('tirmidhi:2406','ur'): "Urdu supplies the whole salvation question and three-part reply. Keep all actual phrasing rather than substituting a familiar translation of the house clause. Only the final footnote glyph and grade are apparatus.",
('tirmidhi:2410','hi'): "Sufyan ibn Abdullah asks for a firm principle, receives the printed Lord/Allah statement and steadfastness instruction, then asks what is feared and sees the tongue gesture. The parenthetical defining Lord as true object of worship is lexical/explanatory context. Preserve the source's imperfect Hindi joins; the final grade/transmission discussion is context.",
('tirmidhi:2410','ur'): "The Urdu dialogue explicitly has holding to the pledge after naming Allah as Lord and then the tongue gesture. Retain its own fuller connective rather than editing Hindi. The '( معبود حقیقی )' definition and two compiler statements remain context.",
('tirmidhi:2413','hi'): "Read the complete Abu Juhayfah account of brotherhood, Salman's visit, meal, sleeping/praying and rights instruction, then the visit to the Prophet and his affirmation. Salman's rights speech is his own Companion speech, separated from the final Prophetic approval in the same incident family. Preserve the printed 'Salman also ate' wording without correcting its apparent participant problem. Compiler grade stays context.",
('tirmidhi:2413','ur'): "Read every Urdu clause, including Umm al-Darda's explanation, fasting meal condition, repeated sleeping instructions, near-dawn prayer and four rights. Salman is the speaker of the rights instruction; the Messenger only supplies the final approval. Keep the duplicated Salman's-eating wording and the source's awkward joining. Footnote glyph and grade are context.",
('tirmidhi:2429','hi'): "The Messenger recites the printed transliterated Quran words and asks about the earth's reports, followed by the companions' response and his explanation. Preserve the actual recitation as narrated speech and the source's abbreviated/defective 'did such acts' clause; do not import the fuller Urdu details. Final grading is context.",
('tirmidhi:2429','ur'): "Urdu provides the Arabic recitation and the explanation that the earth will testify to each man's/woman's deeds on named days. Retain the actual verse words within the narrated recitation and full own explanation; the final grade is context, with no guessed Surah locator added.",
('tirmidhi:2434','hi'): "Read all 5,410 characters of the meat setting and intercession narrative. The speakers Adam/Noah/Abraham/Moses/Jesus and final first-person Messenger account are nested voices within this one narrated report, not newly inferred canonical occurrences. Preserve the source's repeated supplication and defective gate-leaf/geographic wording. Keep the explicit parenthetical return-to-Messenger attribution, which supplies who resumes speaking. The friend definition, Abu Hayyan omitted-three-falsehood pointer, community-label gloss and entry gloss are context, as are final grading/parallels. No absent three falsehoods are filled.",
('tirmidhi:2434','ur'): "Read all 5,061 Urdu characters independently. Preserve every supplied intercession dialogue, request/refusal, the Messenger's repeated community supplication and gate-distance alternatives. The parenthetical marking the Messenger's resumed speech is meaningful actor framing and stays. The Khalil definition, Abu Hayyan pointer to an unprinted list, community-identification gloss, entry gloss, final footnote and compiler tail are context. Retain the distinct Urdu Basra spelling and all source honorifics without normalization.",
('tirmidhi:2454','hi'): "The source reports drawing a square, a protruding central line and side lines, then their human/death/hazard/hope interpretation. The square synonym and parenthetical aspirations explanation are lexical/editorial glosses kept outside narration; preserve the actual later outside-line-as-hope predicate, even where the source's mapping is awkward.",
('tirmidhi:2454','ur'): "Read the complete Urdu drawing report and interpretation. Keep its central-line/person and outside-line/hope wording exactly; do not reconcile the two by rewriting. The square synonym, aspirations gloss and terminal footnote marker are context; the missing closing quote remains a source defect.",
('muslim:2464','hi'): "The entire Hindi field is a same-chain pointer followed by a quotation explicitly said NOT to occur in those transmissions. The quoted religion/arrow wording is negated apparatus, not affirmed transmitted matn here. Read all sibling languages before source-only disposition.",
('muslim:2464','ur'): "Urdu likewise says the arrow/religion phrase is absent from the two transmissions. Preserve that whole negative textual comparison outside narration; do not reverse its negation or use a quoted omission as an affirmative variant.",
('muslim:2464','ar'): "Read both isnads through Jarir and Abu Muawiyah to al-A'mash, then the explicit 'not in their hadith' quoted clause. There is no affirmative body beyond the chain/pointer and negated omission. Entire field remains source-only context.",
('muslim:2464','en'): "The complete English says narrated with the same chain but these quoted words are not there. That is an omitted-word comparison, not an admitted body. The open closing punctuation is preserved rather than completed.",
('muslim:2464','ur-Latn'): "Roman Urdu completely repeats the chain pointer and the statement that the arrow/religion words are not in both reports. No extra affirmative wording exists in this sibling; all five fields support source-only.",
('muslim:2465','hi'): "Ayyub/Muhammad/Ubaydah transmit Ali's description of a Khawarij man's hand and the promise he declines to detail lest the hearers boast. Preserve the simile '(like)', the explicit Ubaydah questioner identification, and the parenthetical promise/direct-hearing scope in the question. Ali's three oath-confirmations attest hearing without supplying the hidden reward. Keep the damaged 'utarnay' word exactly and do not infer the bodily description from another language.",
('muslim:2465','ur'): "Read Ali's entire supplied description, boasting condition, unstated promise and three oath-confirmations. '( جیسے )' limits the resemblance; '( عبیدہ نے )' identifies the questioner; '( وعدہ برا راست )' supplies the source's promise/direct-hearing qualification. Keep these meaningful units and the Urdu spacing/word defects, without inventing the withheld promise content.",
('muslim:2466','hi'): "The field supplies Ubaydah's transmission-assurance formula followed by an unprinted marfu report said to be like Ayyub's. It supplies no underlying report body. Treat the formula and same-as pointer as transmission apparatus, not an independently reconstructed Ali/Prophet account; check all siblings before exclusion.",
('muslim:2466','ur'): "Read the complete Urdu assurance that only what was heard from Ali will be narrated, followed by an omitted report pointer. The Ali parenthesis identifies the source of hearing, but the actual promised body is absent. Preserve all as transmission context.",
('muslim:2466','ar'): "Full isnad and Ubaydah's 'I only narrate what I heard from him' formula are followed by 'then he mentioned from Ali similar to Ayyub's hadith, marfu'. No such body is printed. A chain/formula/pointer does not create the omitted report.",
('muslim:2466','en'): "The short complete English gives Abida's hearing-assurance formula and says he then narrated from him, but supplies nothing narrated. It is transmission framing without a body, consistent with source-only despite a finite metalinguistic formula.",
('muslim:2466','ur-Latn'): "Read all Roman Urdu: Ibn Aun/Muhammad/Ubaydah chain, hearing assurance and same-as-Ayyub marfu pointer only. No independent body appears in the fifth sibling. All five are preserved as reviewed context.",
('muslim:2474','hi'): "The beginning identifies Waki/Shubah and a same-chain comparison; the old 'we do not eat alms' is only the replaced comparator. The expressly transmitted new finite clause 'alms are not lawful for us' is eligible abbreviated wording. Select that supplied new clause with unknown ultimate speaker and no borrowed Prophetic attribution; retain the comparator/chain pointer as context.",
('muslim:2474','ur'): "Urdu explicitly places the old alms wording in an 'instead of' parenthesis and supplies a new affirmative lawful/not-lawful clause. Keep the latter exactly with a transmitted-variant/unknown-speaker qualification. Do not promote the previous-chain pointer or its malformed parenthetical into quotation content.",
('ibnmajah:2449','hi'): "Rafi reports the prohibitions and three categories who may cultivate: own land, given land, and paid lease. The parenthetical gift-or-loan alternatives specify the supplied second category's legal mode rather than merely repeating a word, so retain their source-qualified OR scope. The cash synonym after gold/silver is lexical context. Do not infer which mode is canonically intended.",
('ibnmajah:2449','ur'): "Read all Urdu prohibitions and three cultivating categories. Retain '( ہبہ یا مستعار )' as the source's alternative scope of the given land; it changes which grants are encompassed. '( نقد )' restates gold/silver as cash and is explanatory context. Every other qualification remains exact.",
('ibnmajah:2450','hi'): "Ibn Umar narrates their sharecropping practice, hearing Rafi report the Messenger's prohibition, and abandoning it on that account. Preserve the Companion practice and indirect Prophetic report within one incident family. The parenthetical definition of muzaraat is lexical context, not an additional reported action.",
('ibnmajah:2450','ur'): "The complete Urdu source has the prior practice, no perceived harm, Rafi's reported prohibition and consequent cessation. Keep these voices and cause/order unchanged. The sharecropping gloss and terminal numbered footnote are context; no external agricultural ruling is added.",
}
